WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.000
Wilbur and Orville Wright did everything together.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.120
They started a local newspaper together.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.960
Opened a bike shop together.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.920
And they invented the first airplane together.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.720
They were so in sync that&nbsp;
Wilbur said they “thought

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.480
together”...but they also “fought together”.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.120
They argued A LOT. The bickering&nbsp;
would often start at breakfast

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.480
The bickering would often start at breakfast,&nbsp;
carry on through lunch, and continue past dinner.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.440
Once, a quarrel became so intense&nbsp;
that their sister Katharine cried out,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.160
“If you don’t stop arguing, I’ll leave home!”

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.200
Standing up for themselves was&nbsp;
encouraged in their childhood.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.080
Their father, Milton Wright, was a bishop,&nbsp;
but he included books by atheists and

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.640
agnostics in his library and encouraged&nbsp;
his children to read and debate them.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.800
Debating helped them develop the&nbsp;
courage to fight for their ideas.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.800
If they hadn’t, the airplane&nbsp;
might never have been invented.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.440
In the early days of aviation, the very idea&nbsp;
of human flight was considered impossible.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.400
The Washington Post declared at the turn of the&nbsp;
20th century: “It is a fact that man can’t fly.”

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.000
Many attempted controlled flight 
but none had succeeded.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.040
In 1896, German aviator Otto Lilienthal lost&nbsp;
his life when he lost control of his glider

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.240
due to a sudden gust of wind. 
 
His technique of adjusting the

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.360
machine's center of gravity by shifting&nbsp;
his body proved impractical and dangerous.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.440
Despite no college education&nbsp;
or technical training,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.440
the Wright brothers dared to dream&nbsp;
of flight, with Orville saying:

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.560
“We could not understand that there was&nbsp;
anything about a bird that would enable

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.520
it to fly that could not be built&nbsp;
on a larger scale and used by man.”

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.000
One of their most intense arguments had&nbsp;
to do with the design of the propeller.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.200
In the early 20th century,&nbsp;
there were no existing models.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.040
Most of what they knew came&nbsp;
from their knowledge of boats,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.120
but a propeller designed for the water&nbsp;
didn’t work efficiently in the air.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.320
They needed a narrower blade with a higher speed.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.080
With no existing designs to work from, they&nbsp;
had to invent the propeller themselves.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.240
They argued over the propeller’s speed,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.840
the precise angle at which the blades strike&nbsp;
the air, the velocity of their flying machine,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.760
and how airflow around the propeller&nbsp;
blades affects aerodynamics.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.520
All of these elements were&nbsp;
deeply interconnected and

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.480
challenged them to engineer the ideal propeller.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.840
Wilbur, the older brother, was&nbsp;
highly disagreeable and didn’t

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.920
care much about what other people thought of him.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.840
Orville, on the other hand, was gentle and&nbsp;
sensitive to criticism but wasn’t afraid to punch

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.240
back when it came to his older brother. Wilbur&nbsp;
even exclaimed, “He’s such a good scrapper.”

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.000
Author John McMahon, who wrote a&nbsp;
book about the Wright brothers,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.640
described their intense debates this way:

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.880
“Their low staccato voices sounded like muffled&nbsp;
machine guns in the low-ceilinged living room

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.480
of their home and in the little bedrooms&nbsp;
upstairs, where they continued to hurl angles,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.320
sines, cosines and tangents at each&nbsp;
other through the thin partitions.”

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.680
Sometimes, these debates&nbsp;
escalated into shouting matches.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.240
Their bike mechanic Charlie Taylor who helped&nbsp;
build the engines for their planes, observed:

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.720
“They'd shout at each other something terrible. I

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.960
don't think they really got mad,&nbsp;
but they sure got awfully hot.”

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.520
Yet, they never attacked each&nbsp;
other personally but rather

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.320
focused on challenging each other’s viewpoints.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.280
They’d even find themselves persuading each&nbsp;
other and then both changing their minds.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:20.000
Orville recalled: “...we would discover that we&nbsp;
were as far from agreement as when we started,

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.840
but that both had changed to the other's&nbsp;
original position in the discussion.”

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.000
After months of debate, the Wright&nbsp;
brothers finally understood that

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.400
a propeller works like a rotating wing.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.000
When the propellers spin and push air backward,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.160
it creates a force called thrust,&nbsp;
which pushes the airplane forward.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.560
This is based on Newton's third law of motion,

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.800
which says that for every action,&nbsp;
there's an equal and opposite reaction.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.280
They eventually figured out that&nbsp;
the plane needed not one propeller,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.080
but two spinning in opposite directions.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.680
The spinning of one would balance out the&nbsp;
other… making the plane easier to control.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.440
They made the propellers out of wood,

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.600
cutting them to the right size and&nbsp;
shape using a hatchet and drawshave.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.720
They then decided to put them&nbsp;
to the test at Kitty Hawk,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.880
a small fishing village in North&nbsp;
Carolina known for its strong winds.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:12.680
When they had to decide who would attempt&nbsp;
the first flight on December 14, 1903,

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.440
they avoided another argument&nbsp;
in this case by tossing a coin.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.480
Wilbur won.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.520
But he lost the chance to be the first&nbsp;
to fly successfully when he oversteered,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.200
causing the aircraft to climb too&nbsp;
steeply, stall, and dive into the sand.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.600
After repairing the Flyer, they made&nbsp;
a second attempt three days later.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:34.280
With Orville in the pilot’s seat, the&nbsp;
flyer stayed in the air for 12 seconds,

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.400
traveling 120 ft or 37 meters.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:43.200
When Orville was later asked if he was scared,&nbsp;
he responded: “Scared? There wasn’t time.”

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.120
They flew four times that day.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.640
The fourth and final attempt – this&nbsp;
time with Wilbur at the controls - was

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:55.480
the best flight, lasting 59 seconds&nbsp;
and covering 852 feet (260 meters).

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.760
They had taught the world that humans could fly.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.880
But they didn’t really celebrate.&nbsp;
For them, it was business as usual.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.880
Their mechanic, Charlie Taylor, captured&nbsp;
their understated reaction perfectly:

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.760
“I know I thought it was pretty nice that they&nbsp;
had done what they set out to do, and I was glad

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.480
to hear that the motor ran all right. But&nbsp;
I don't remember doing any jig steps. The

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.400
boys were always so matter-of-fact about things,&nbsp;
and they never made an effort to get me excited.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.280
Even when they got home, there was no&nbsp;
special celebration in the shop. Of

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.080
course they were pleased with the flights.&nbsp;
But their first word with me, as I remember,

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.240
was about the motor being damaged…They were&nbsp;
always thinking of the next thing to do…”

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.960
Just as their arguments were never about&nbsp;
personally attacking each other but about

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:42.160
making their planes better, they didn’t celebrate&nbsp;
but instead, focused on improving their aircraft.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.000
Now they had to prove they could fully&nbsp;
control their plane by turning and flying

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.720
longer distances as their flights up to&nbsp;
then were short and in a straight line.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.920
Luckily, the owner of a cow pasture a few&nbsp;
miles outside of their hometown of Dayton,

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:59.480
Ohio, kindly let them fly there so they didn’t&nbsp;
have to make the long trek to Kitty Hawk.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:05.240
In 1904 and 1905, the Wright&nbsp;
brothers made about 150 flights.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:10.440
One lasted 39 minutes,&nbsp;
covering 24.5 miles or 39 km,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.680
during which Wilbur circled the field 29 times.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.360
When it was time to show the world&nbsp;
their invention, the French aviator

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.640
Léon Delagrange summed up the superiority&nbsp;
of the Wright flying machine this way:

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.680
“Nous sommes battus”, meaning, “We are beaten.”

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:31.480
Their rigorous debates propelled the&nbsp;
Wright brothers to conquer the skies.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:36.200
As Wilbur once said: “Discussion brings&nbsp;
out new ways of looking at things.”

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.680
If the Wright brothers hadn’t argued so&nbsp;
much, they would never have pushed each

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.880
other to try new things, and somebody else&nbsp;
may have ended up in the history books.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.600
Their dedication and knowledge in math and physics&nbsp;
led to the world’s first functional airplane.

